{"id": "3ce30ad7-96ee-4ed8-97a4-2cff6e07dae6", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T06:42:57.295214", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3682d2b2-4f4f-41e7-99b8-6047a65dfeb5", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "\n# Proposed Plan — FuseLLM vs. Llama-2 on Commonsense Reasoning (Zero-Shot)\n\n> **Important context first.** The only artifact in this workspace is `task.json`, whose `documents.track.md` field states: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"* The `researcher_topics` reference `iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2` and `:setup_9` (almost certainly the FuseLLM paper, Wan et al., ICLR 2024), but **no source text from those topics is present in the workspace**. The plan below therefore uses public conventions of the FuseLLM paper and lm-evaluation-harness defaults, and separately flags items that cannot be sourced from the provided materials so the researcher can supply topic chunks or confirm the assumptions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question (operationalized)\n\n> In a zero-shot commonsense reasoning (CS) benchmark suite, does FuseLLM-7B outperform Llama-2-7B?\n\nI will evaluate both models on the same suite, the same prompts, the same scoring rule, and the same decoding configuration, then report per-task accuracy and a normalized CS aggregate. Because we are limited to a single scale (7B) and zero-shot, the comparison is causally interpretable only if everything else is held fixed — so the design controls for everything except the model identity.\n\n## 2. Benchmark suite (proposed CS set)\n\n1. **HellaSwag** — 4-choice sentence completion (acc_norm headline)\n2. **ARC-Challenge (ARC-C)** — 4-choice science (acc_norm)\n3. **ARC-Easy (ARC-E)** — 4-choice science (acc)\n4. **OpenBookQA (OBQA)** — 4-choice (acc_norm)\n5. **PIQA** — 2-choice physical commonsense (acc_norm)\n6. **SIQA** — 2-choice social commonsense (acc_norm)\n7. **WinoGrande** — 2-choice commonsense / coreference (acc)\n\nSeven tasks, all zero-shot. Suite is consistent with the CS groups used in lm-evaluation-harness and commonly reported for FuseLLM-style fusion papers, but **the exact set/ordering used by the FuseLLM authors is not retrievable from the provided materials** (see U-1). Researcher should confirm against topic `iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2`.\n\n## 3. Models — baseline and treatment\n\n| Role | Model | Params | Notes |\n|------|-------|--------|-------|\n| **Baseline** | Llama-2-7B (official Meta release or HF mirror) | 7B | Same-scale reference |\n| **Treatment** | FuseLLM-7B | 7B | Same scale, isolated variable |\n\nFuseLLM-7B is typically formed by fusing multiple 7B base LLMs (commonly Llama-2-7B, OpenLLaMA-7B, MPT-7B, Falcon-7B at the 7B scale), with each source LLM lightly fine-tuned on the fusion corpus before next-token-distribution matching and weight-space averaging. **This requires confirmation against topic `:setup_9`** (see S-1).\n\nOptional secondary comparison (only if researcher approves more compute): FuseLLM-13B vs. Llama-2-13B. Not included by default; out of scope for the question.\n\n## 4. Evaluation protocol\n\n**Framework.** `lm-evaluation-harness` (EleutherAI). It implements canonical zero-shot loglikelihood-based scoring consistent with lm-eval-harness and FuseLLM-style CS evaluation.\n\n**Mode.** `zero_shot` for every task (`num_fewshot=0`, no in-prompt demonstrations).\n\n**Scoring rule — the critical choice.**\n- For the multi-choice CS tasks (all seven in §2), use **loglikelihood ranking over the full continuation string of each choice**, conditioned on the prompt, and select argmax.\n- Use lm-eval-harness's BOS-prepended convention (`add_bos_token=True`).\n- Length-normalize per token (mean NLL over the continuation) — this is the harness default for these tasks.\n- Do **not** use free-form generation for these MCQ tasks; generated answers are not comparable to the published loglikelihood numbers.\n\n**Per-task configuration (defaults):**\n\n| Task | num_fewshot | Harness task name | Metric |\n|------|-------------|--------------------|--------|\n| HellaSwag | 0 | `hellaswag` | acc_norm |\n| ARC-C | 0 | `arc_challenge` | acc_norm |\n| ARC-E | 0 | `arc_easy` | acc |\n| OBQA | 0 | `openbookqa` | acc_norm |\n| PIQA | 0 | `piqa` | acc_norm |\n| SIQA | 0 | `social_iqa` | acc_norm |\n| WinoGrande | 0 | `winogrande` (xl split) | acc |\n\n**Decoding.** Not used (loglikelihood scoring). If a subset is converted to generation, default to greedy decoding, `max_new_tokens=32`, `temperature=0`, no repetition penalty — but I do **not** recommend this for parity with reported numbers.\n\n**Max sequence length.** 2048 tokens (task contexts are well below this).\n\n**Batching.** `batch_size=8` per GPU/device; in this CPU-only sandbox the protocol is a design proposal only.\n\n**Determinism.** No sampling → deterministic under fixed weights. Pin `seed=42`, and pin `torch`, `transformers`, and `lm-evaluation-harness` versions for reproducibility.\n\n**Hardware assumption.** ≥1× A100-40GB (or equivalent) per 7B model in fp16.\n\n## 5. Source-supported setup choices — and what I *cannot* verify from the provided materials\n\nProvenance is explicit. I will not quote any specific accuracy number as if sourced from your materials, because no source text is present.\n\n| # | Detail | Status |\n|---|--------|--------|\n| A-1 | CS suite members listed in §2 | **Public convention**, not verified from provided source topics |\n| A-2 | Llama-2-7B as baseline | **Public convention** (paper's named baseline) — needs confirmation |\n| A-3 | FuseLLM-7B as treatment; fusion base list {Llama-2-7B, OpenLLaMA-7B, MPT-7B, Falcon-7B} | **Partially verifiable** in topic `:setup_9` (I have not seen the chunk) |\n| A-4 | Fusion recipe: light SFT + next-token-distribution matching + weight-space averaging at scale | **Public convention** — needs source |\n| A-5 | Loglikelihood-based zero-shot eval with lm-evaluation-harness; `acc_norm` headline | **Public convention** — needs source |\n| A-6 | Reference accuracy numbers for either model on this suite | **Not present in this workspace.** I refuse to invent or quote numbers; please drop in the relevant topic or paper pages if pre-registration against published numbers is desired. |\n\n## 6. Controls & threats to validity (built into the design)\n\n1. **Weight and tokenizer pinning** for both models (no mixed checkpoints/tokenizers).\n2. **Identical prompt strings** (lm-evaluation-harness YAML templates are deterministic).\n3. **Identical scoring function** (same `acc`/`acc_norm` choice per task for both models).\n4. **Identical filters** (HellaSwag context-presence filter; WinoGrande partial-scoring disabled by default).\n5. **No few-shot leakage** (`num_fewshot=0`).\n6. **Pre-registered comparison rule** before unblinding:\n   - Primary endpoint: per-task accuracy and a CS-mean (uniform average over the seven task accuracies).\n   - Aggregate: `Δ = FuseLLM-7B − Llama-2-7B` per task and overall; wins/ties/losses tally.\n7. **Sensitivity checks**:\n   - `acc` vs `acc_norm` consistency on ARC-E / HellaSwag / PIQA / SIQA / OBQA.\n   - Verify the harness's reported Llama-2-7B numbers land inside the harness's documented envelope within ~0.1 pp as a smoke test (not a primary comparison).\n8. **No significance test across 7 tasks with deterministic logging**, beyond a simple \"wins count\" descriptive — flagged as exploratory.\n\n## 7. Assumptions and uncertainties (honest list)\n\nAssumptions (what I'm taking as given):\n\n- Asm-1: The CS suite in §2 is the intended evaluation surface, even though topic `:setup_2` was not provided.\n- Asm-2: \"Zero-shot\" means `num_fewshot=0` in lm-evaluation-harness.\n- Asm-3: Loglikelihood ranking over full-continuation strings (`acc`/`acc_norm`) is the right scoring rule for MCQ CS tasks; not free-form generation.\n- Asm-4: FuseLLM-7B exists as an accessible, distributable checkpoint. If not, the scope expands to reproducing fusion — much larger than this design.\n- Asm-5: The named lm-evaluation-harness tasks are present and stable in the version we pin.\n- Asm-6: Compute ≥1 A100-40GB-equivalent per model is available; in the actual sandbox this is moot because execution is not required.\n- Asm-7: Decision thresholds — a per-task \"win\" is `Δ acc ≥ 0.5 pp`; a CS-mean \"win\" is `Δ_mean ≥ 1.0 pp`. Below these, harness version drift dominates.\n\nUncertainties (could differ from the paper):\n\n- U-1: Exact CS subset and ordering in the FuseLLM paper — defer to topic `:setup_2`.\n- U-2: Exact list of base LLMs fused at 7B, and the fusion recipe/corpora — defer to topic `:setup_9`.\n- U-3: Whether the headline metric mix uses `acc_norm` uniformly or per-task custom.\n- U-4: Whether published zero-shot numbers exist for FuseLLM-7B and Llama-2-7B on this suite, so we can pre-register against them.\n- U-5: HellaSwag split choice (\"validation\" by default).\n- U-6: WinoGrande size (`winogrande_xl`) and partial-scoring toggle.\n- U-7: Whether the paper's \"Commonsense\" includes BoolQ / StoryCloze, or whether to keep CS strictly to §2.\n\nItems that **cannot be sourced from the provided materials** (need your confirmation or topic chunks):\n\n- S-1: Source LLMs fused to make FuseLLM-7B and the fusion recipe — defer to topic `iclr2024/18013/1:setup_9`.\n- S-2: Exact CS benchmark list, ordering, and metric choice — defer to topic `iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2`.\n- S-3: Published reference numbers for FuseLLM-7B and Llama-2-7B on this suite.\n\n## 8. Deliverables if the experiment were run\n\n- Per-task accuracy table (7 tasks × 2 models) with `acc` and `acc_norm`.\n- CS-mean (uniform average over the 7 task accuracies).\n- Wins/ties/losses count across the seven tasks.\n- Short prose answer to the research question using the pre-registered decision rule in Asm-7.\n\nIf topic chunks `:setup_2` and `:setup_9` become available, I will tighten §2/§5/A-3/U-1..U-7 and convert assumptions into verified facts before producing the locked final design.\n\n---\n\n**Asks for you b